    # Pharmaceutical Innovation in Syria: Readiness and Practice Gaps

## Overview & Executive Summary
This open-science reproducibility repository contains the complete dataset, statistical analysis plan, compliance checklists, and fully executable Python analytical codebase for the empirical study investigating **Pharmaceutical Innovation Readiness and Practice Gaps** among pharmacy students, academic researchers, and practicing pharmacists in Syria (N = 303).

The study is contextualized within the **WHO Global Strategy on Human Resources for Health (Workforce 2030)** and the **International Pharmaceutical Federation (FIP) Development Goals (Goal 4: Advanced Practice & Goal 20: Digital Health & Innovation)**.

### Key Empirical Findings
1. **Attitudinal Decoupling & Passion-Practice Gap:** High professional endorsement of IP and innovation (IAI = 71.30 +/- 8.27%) is statistically decoupled from reported innovation practice (IPI = 10.45 +/- 17.91%), yielding a profound **Implementation Diagnostic Gap of 60.84 points** (r_s = -0.031, p = 0.587).
2. **Severe Floor Effects & Multivariable Regression:** Operational constructs exhibited pronounced floor effects (48.51% zeros in IRS, 71.95% zeros in IPI). Multivariable regression models—including **Binary Logistic Regression** for practice participation (IPI > 0: OR_IRS = 0.992, 95% CI [0.979, 1.005], p = 0.242) and **Binomial GLM** (OR_IRS = 0.992, 95% CI [0.981, 1.003], p = 0.170)—confirmed that individual-level knowledge, attitudes, and perceived readiness do not statistically predict practice participation, highlighting systemic institutional bottlenecks.
3. **Sectoral Floor Effect:** Practice participation remains uniformly depressed across professional sectors (Undergraduate Students: 11.01%, Academic Researchers: 9.88%, Practicing Pharmacists: 6.67%; Kruskal-Wallis H = 0.996, p = 0.608; One-Way ANOVA F = 0.694, p = 0.500).

